{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Avenue Supermarts Cash Conversion Cycle Analysis",
  "scenario": "You are analysing Avenue Supermarts (DMart) for a Mumbai equity desk. The company's negative cash conversion cycle is a key structural advantage — it collects from customers before paying suppliers, funding growth internally. Build the full CCC calculation from historical balance-sheet and P&L data to understand how DMart achieves this superpower and compare it to a competitor with a positive CCC.",
  "instructions": "Calculate Days Inventory Outstanding (DIO), Days Sales Outstanding (DSO), Days Payable Outstanding (DPO), and the Cash Conversion Cycle (CCC) for DMart and a competitor; then interpret which business model is capital-efficient and identify the key driver of each company's working capital profile.",
  "learning_objective": "Compute DIO, DSO, DPO, and CCC for two retailers; identify whether each has a negative or positive cycle; explain which driver (inventory, receivables, or payables) is the key efficiency lever; and assess the strategic implications for growth funding.",
  "hints": [
    "DIO = (Average Inventory ÷ COGS) × 365. Use AVERAGE() for opening + closing inventory.",
    "CCC = DIO + DSO − DPO. Subtract DPO because delaying payment *frees* cash (reduces the trap).",
    "A negative CCC means the company collects before it pays — no working-capital funding needed. Look for which company has very low DSO (fast collections) and higher DPO (delayed payments)."
  ],
